WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.044 --> 00:00:04.755
♪♪

00:00:04.755 --> 00:00:05.964
Hi guys!

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:07.299
I am Krys Marshall,

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:08.967
I play Danielle Poole

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:10.302
on For All Mankind.

00:00:10.469 --> 00:00:13.555
And today you guys
are in for a total treat,

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.682
because I am gonna
give you a tour

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.017
of the NASA HAB...

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.185
AKA...

00:00:18.185 --> 00:00:19.186
The Happy Valley.

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:27.694
First thing's up is

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.363
our very cool
computer system here.

00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:31.198
This is where
you will see our

00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:33.951
astronauts receive phone calls

00:00:33.951 --> 00:00:36.036
and video calls from home.

00:00:36.078 --> 00:00:38.038
This very archaic piece

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.415
of equipment is early FaceTime.

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:43.126
As we continue back
you’ll see there’s

00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:47.965
bunks here, however... 

00:00:47.965 --> 00:00:50.050
if we have more
than eight astronauts

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.761
then we actually
sleep in shifts.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:55.055
They call that “hot racking”

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:55.931
in the biz.

00:00:55.931 --> 00:00:57.391
This is our tunnel

00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:00.644
which leads to the
next half of the HAB.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.480
Our crew has done
an immaculate job

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.607
of building our sets
so they feel

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:06.608
really real.

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:08.318
Very sturdy.

00:01:08.944 --> 00:01:09.987
Little secret here;

00:01:09.987 --> 00:01:11.446
there was an earthquake

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:12.781
in the middle of the take.

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:13.407
I went...

00:01:14.408 --> 00:01:15.701
[laughs]

00:01:15.826 --> 00:01:16.827
Come through this way.

00:01:17.286 --> 00:01:18.829
We have our airlock.

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:20.872
So you’ll see that
each of these

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:22.332
separate sections are
kind of like

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:23.583
these massive chambers.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.711
God forbid there is a leak,

00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:27.129
we can shut off the chamber

00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:29.381
and the thing stays
vacuum sealed,

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.300
like, high quality
tupperware.

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:33.051
Come on down this way guys.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.095
Here, we will have spare tires,

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.221
we got batteries, we got

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:39.099
everything that one needs
for life on Mars.

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:42.019
This is the — maybe the
most important door

00:01:42.019 --> 00:01:43.645
in the whole,
entire Mars HAB

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.106
because on
the other side...

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:53.739
That’s not just the
miracle of T.V.

00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:54.364
that’s real.

00:01:54.573 --> 00:01:55.741
We’re heading to the galley.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:58.327
There are foods

00:01:58.327 --> 00:01:59.619
and knick-knacks

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:00.662
and MRE's

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.331
which is short for
meals ready to eat.

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:04.833
We enjoy some really
delicious things here.

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:06.835
Things like: gelatinous meat,

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:09.755
meat stew, meat slivers,
meat slices,

00:02:09.755 --> 00:02:13.550
so really, really vile
things that we eat here.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.094
But, we also do our
science experiments here

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:16.970
and things like that.

00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:18.263
This is our seismograph.

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:19.473
This tells us what’s going on

00:02:19.473 --> 00:02:20.891
on the surface of Mars here.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.063
The last and final
space, which is 

00:02:26.938 --> 00:02:27.981
very dear to my heart

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.150
because it’s the only
living thing—truly

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:30.984
living thing here,

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:33.528
other than the
astronauts and cosmonauts —

00:02:33.528 --> 00:02:34.363
our garden.

00:02:34.613 --> 00:02:35.822
Or as we like to call it,

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:36.823
“The Swamp.”

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:38.367
We’re growing
some gorgeous

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:39.868
red-vine tomatoes

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:40.994
and kale

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:42.454
and dark leafy greens

00:02:42.454 --> 00:02:43.455
and things like that.

00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:47.334
And on that note,

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:48.752
it’s been nice
having ya.

00:02:48.919 --> 00:02:50.295
Ta-ta for now.

00:02:51.922 --> 00:02:53.548
♪♪

